\cf2 Marshwiggle:\
Ezekiel has many times, as for instance in chapter 7, warned that the LORD will judge the people of Israel according to their ways. Similarly, as in chapter 9, the LORD warned that he would bring their deeds on their heads. That is, the LORD's ways will be according to their ways. There will be punishment, but that punishment will not be arbitrary, but rather entirely deserved because of what they have done.\
Thus, in chapter 16, the LORD articulates his ways at length by articulating the ways of the people of Israel at length, in the longest oracle in the Hebrew Bible. The ways of the people are an abomination to the LORD, thus, the oracle begins with instructions to Ezekiel to make known to Jerusalem her abominations.\
But, as Ezekiel implied in chapter 4, there has been a centuries long history of misdeeds. And so, to make known those abominations, Ezekiel has to go back to early in Israel's history. How does his retelling of the story of Israel differ from the story his original audience would have wanted to hear, and even from the history as told by other parts of the Bible? And, perhaps more importantly, why?\
Opus:\
Looking at Ezekiel 16, the retelling deliberately strips away every source of pride and self-justification the exiles might have clung to.\
The most jarring difference: Jerusalem has Canaanite parentage - "your father was an Amorite and your mother a Hittite" (v.3). This directly contradicts the patriarchal narratives where Abraham leaves Mesopotamia and explicitly rejects Canaanite connections for his son's marriage. The audience would expect their story to begin with divine election of Abraham, the promises to the patriarchs, perhaps the Exodus. Instead, Ezekiel presents them as indigenous to the land they're supposed to have conquered, children of the very peoples they were commanded to destroy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0
