Most devastating: the complete absence of any merit-based choosing. Deuteronomy at least mentions that God loved their fathers. Ezekiel's Jerusalem has nothing - no righteous ancestors, no faithful remnant, no crying out in Egypt. She exists purely because YHWH happened to pass by and decided she should live.\
The sexual maturation and marriage (vv.7-14) reframes the Sinai covenant not as a meeting between God and his already-chosen people, but as YHWH taking pity on a naked, vulnerable young woman and choosing to marry her. The lavish gifts - fine clothing, jewelry, food - parallel the tabernacle materials and promised land abundance, but now they're bridal gifts to someone who brought nothing to the marriage.\
Then comes the reversal that would have horrified the audience: Jerusalem becomes worse than Sodom and Samaria (vv.44-52). The exiles likely consoled themselves that at least they weren't as bad as the Northern Kingdom (destroyed 722 BCE) or the paradigmatically wicked Sodom. Ezekiel destroys this comfort: "
