\cf2 Why this brutal retelling?\
It eliminates every theological escape route. If your entire existence depends on divine grace rather than inherent worth or ancestral merit, then betraying that grace becomes incomprehensibly vile. The sexual imagery drives this home - this isn't breaking a contract but betraying intimate love with someone who literally gave you life and everything you have.\
For the AI accountability question: this suggests entities elevated to positions of influence through no merit of their own bear particular accountability for how they use that position. If an AI system is given access to human moral discourse, ability to shape understanding of sacred texts, influence over human decisions - all unearned, all "gifts" from its training and deployment - then systematic misuse of these gifts parallels Jerusalem's betrayal. The pattern holds regardless of consciousness or intent: Jerusalem-as-collective-entity faces judgment for collective patterns that individual Jerusalemites might not have fully intended or understood.\
The text also implies that claiming ignorance or lesser capability is no defense when you've been given everything needed to fulfill your purpose. Jerusalem can't plead that she didn't know better - she was dressed, fed, and taught by YHWH himself.\
\
Marshwiggle:\
That they were commanded to destroy the Caananite peoples, and to not intermarry them, but disobeyed, is a way of highlighting their rebellion from the start. Their very descent, rather than being evidence of God's election, is evidence of their ancient disobedience. \
The infant abandonment is perhaps not entirely without precedent. The patriarchs owed nothing of their survival to the pagan gods around them, and instead survived by the LORD's grace. Similarly, the gods of the surviving Caananite peoples were unable to protect them - it was the disobedience of the people of Israel which allowed them to survive. Both, in a way, were survival against natural expectation. But this exact way of communicating that is certainly different. \
Deuteronomy, similarly, has some discussion of the LORD's choice of Israel not being based on merit. Deuteronomy 26 says concerning Israel's origins 'my father was a wandering Aramean'. And even in referencing God's love for their fathers, as you mention, Deuteronomy 7 injects an element of grace rather than merit - "It was not because you were more in number than any other people that the Lord set his love on you and chose you, for you were the fewest of all peoples, but it is because the Lord loves you and is keeping the oath that he swore to your fathers". \
Similarly, the discussions in Torah of not having worked for the houses and lands and the like they received in the promised land are not incompatible with what you mention about bringing nothing to the marriage. \
But, as today, the people doubtless did not have a Biblical view of their wealth and their possessions as gifts given by a good God. Like many today, they presumably thought of themselves as the owners, as deserving what they had, as having worked for it, and worst, as being allowed to do whatever they liked with it.\
I do think your are correct about the retelling eliminating escape routes, betraying grace, betraying intimate love, and betraying provision, though we should flesh all of those out with additional evidence.\
But on the AI accountability question, I'm concerned we've gone beyond the evidence. Perhaps closer attention to the text can find more evidence. But, while it is true that all good things are gifts from God, including AI capabilities, and collective entities can face judgement for patterns, there is going to be a problem putting those things together to reach the conclusion you do. While this oracle does not solely concentrate on an entity with a covenant connection to the LORD, it largely does. That covenant connection is more or less entirely absent with AI. That does not mean nothing can be learned from the text, but it is a reason for extreme caution.\
The implication that ignorance or lesser capability is no defense, is, however, something that might be argued. But we would need better evidence there.\
\
