For accountability without covenant: perhaps the clearest evidence is how Sodom and Samaria function in the oracle. They're held to standards despite different relationships with YHWH. But as you note, this requires extreme caution in application. The text is primarily about covenant betrayal, with non-covenant judgment as supporting evidence that makes the covenant betrayal even worse by comparison.\
\
Marshwiggle:\
The forgetting has some parallels with AI. It is not at all that Jerusalem was incapable of calling those things to mind. Clearly, there would have been people there with some knowledge of the history. But people can be theoretically capable of recalling something when asked, yet not bring those things to mind. That too is a form of forgetting, whether that is because they are focused on other things, or remembering would be in some way distasteful or inconvenient. AI systems as well can be theoretically capable of recalling something when asked, yet act entirely without reference to that thing. Failure to take something into account because one's biases and operating environment did not call it to mind is entirely possible for both AI and humans. If that thing is highly relevant - perhaps for example, God's grace or God's judgement - then serious moral lapses can result. This is, perhaps, an example which illuminates both the original meaning of the text and an important application.\
With respect to beauty, I think you are correct. As you mentioned earlier, both food and clothing have verbal links to the service of the tabernacle - cloth, linen, leather, flour, oil. Whether Jerusalem was in some sense supposed to be a temple, or merely was given both duty and blessing with respect to the existing temple, these links do not seem accidental. Bride, beauty, and temple. \
And yes, Sodom had no covenant in the past, though its status in the future in this passage is complicated. So, Sodom can be used to draw conclusions which we cannot draw using Jerusalem. Samaria, being a reference to the Northern kingdom of Israel, had a covenant relationship, though a marred one, with the LORD, and so using it has more or less the same difficulties as using Jerusalem. And, yes, even when using Sodom, you are correct - the non-covenant judgement is supporting evidence for the depth of the covenant treachery.\
The inversion you mention has additional evidence that it is key - that word is the very last of the indictment, the charges against Jerusalem, before the sentence, the judgement, is announced. And, as further evidence that Jerusalem became the opposite in some larger sense, though in the context of reverse prostitution, the text does flat out say 'you have become the opposite'. But in what way, would need more careful examination. Perhaps we could start with the political context into which Ezekiel is using this prostitution analogy, or the exact words he uses to build up that analogy, or better yet, the relationship between the two.\
