Taking another husband, or at least, taking a man in place of her husband, is directly confirmed by verse 32, "Adulterous wife, who receives strangers instead of her husband."\
And yes, both Biblical and extrabiblical evidence confirms the compulsive nature of this adultery. That not being satisfied confirms the accusation that the adulterous wife was given everything, yet chose to seek satisfaction elsewhere. No other man could satisfy, so there was not at all good reason to be unfaithful.\
Though on the political level the human muscle you mention is a large army and the like, on the adultery metaphor level, that big flesh is likely to be a rather cruder reason to commit adultery. Still, on the political level, failing to recognize that the LORD made heaven and earth and so needs no army, failing to know that the LORD's heavenly army vastly outstrips any earthly army, failing to remember that the LORD himself is a warrior and defeated the Egyptian army and the Assyrian army personally and utterly - these are serious failures, rebellious failures. If one's husband has defeated even great armies, but one still sleeps with another man to get protection - that goes beyond forgetful or ungrateful to outright rebellious. \
I am not sure why you are referring to high places though. The word for high places is specifically not used, meaning we are not, as in some earlier chapters, referring to pagan rituals on hills. Instead, this is an urban phenomenon. This may be a reference to some sort of foreign cult installation - a raised platform for worship of other gods is not out of the question. But this adulterous wife going out of her way to make sure everyone sees her spreading her legs may be the meaning.\
