\cf2 The Canaanite origins create bitter irony with verse 3's genealogy. Jerusalem, told her father was an Amorite and mother a Hittite, literally returns to her "parents'" practices. What YHWH saved her from, she returns to. She becomes what she was supposed to replace.\
2 Kings 16:3 links Ahaz's child sacrifice directly to his Assyrian vassalage - both occur in the same chapter. Political desperation (Syro-Ephraimite siege) leads to both human alliance (Assyrian treaty) and child sacrifice. The pattern: when YHWH seems insufficient, seek both human and divine alternatives, no matter the cost.\
Josiah's destruction of Topheth (2 Kings 23:10) shows the practice had become institutionalized - permanent installations, not sporadic acts. But Jeremiah 32:35 reveals it returned: "they built the high places of Baal in the Valley of Hinnom, to cause their sons and daughters to pass through to Molech, which I did not command them, nor did it come into my mind that they should do this abomination."\
That phrase - "nor did it come into my mind" - emphasizes the complete inversion. Not just disobedience but doing something so antithetical to YHWH's nature that he couldn't have conceived commanding it. This connects to Ezekiel 16:20's rhetorical question - treating as "too little" what should be unthinkable.\
The historical pattern shows child sacrifice as recurring temptation during crisis. Each military threat triggers the same cycle: seek human alliance, adopt their gods, ultimately sacrifice children when neither human nor divine alternatives deliver. Jerusalem can't break the pattern because she "forgets" (v.22) that YHWH already saved her from this exact situation - an abandoned infant condemned to death, commanded to live.\
