\cf2 <notes> Critical difference: Hosea's unfaithful wife is deceived; Ezekiel's is calculating Hosea 2:8 - "She did not know that I gave her grain, wine, and oil" Ezekiel repeatedly emphasizes Jerusalem knew: "which I gave you," "my oil," "my incense" </notes>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Jeremiah 3 introduces the sister comparison that Ezekiel expands. Jeremiah 3:6-11 describes Israel and Judah as faithless sisters, with Judah being worse because she saw Israel's punishment and didn't learn. Ezekiel develops this into the triple comparison with Samaria and Sodom, adding the devastating assessment that Jerusalem justified her sisters' behavior by being worse (16:51-52).\
Jeremiah raises the legal problem: "If a man divorces his wife and she goes from him and becomes another man's wife, will he return to her?" (Jer. 3:1). By Torah law (Deut. 24:1-4), remarriage after adultery is impossible. Ezekiel embraces this impossibility - there's no call to return, no invitation to repent. The restoration comes entirely from YHWH's action despite Jerusalem's irredeemable state.\
Most significantly, Jeremiah 3:19-20 has YHWH lamenting: "I said, 'How I would set you among my sons, and give you a pleasant land'... But surely, as a wife treacherously leaves her husband, so have you been treacherous to me." This parental language anticipates Ezekiel's fusion of parent-child and husband-wife relationships. YHWH is simultaneously father (giving inheritance) and husband (being betrayed).\
Ezekiel synthesizes and extremifies both predecessors: Hosea's prostitution metaphor plus Jeremiah's legal impossibility plus new elements (infant exposure, child sacrifice, payment reversal). The audience knowing these earlier texts would recognize Ezekiel's version as categorically worse - not confused (Hosea) or treacherous (Jeremiah) but systematically perverse, inverting every aspect of the relationship.\
The cumulative effect: Ezekiel's audience can't comfort themselves that they're in Hosea's restorable situation or even Jeremiah's difficult-but-negotiable position. They've exceeded all prophetic categories for unfaithfulness.\
