\cf2 The sister comparison forces uncomfortable recognition. If YHWH lacked power, how did Samaria fall in 722 BCE after their idolatry? How did Sodom's destruction enter universal memory? The exiles can't simultaneously claim YHWH destroyed those cities for sin but couldn't prevent Babylon from destroying Jerusalem.\
Most cunningly, the oracle's description matches what actually happened too precisely for coincidence. The stripping, the taking of jewelry (temple vessels), the burning of houses, the sword - Ezekiel describes exactly what Babylon did. Either YHWH orchestrated these precise fulfillments or Ezekiel got impossibly lucky predicting details. The exiles experiencing these exact events can't dismiss them as generic threats.\
The "payment reversal" detail particularly undermines the weakness theory. Jerusalem exhausted her treasury paying Egypt and others for protection that failed. If YHWH were weak, wouldn't these human powers have been strong? But the oracle exposes that Jerusalem's chosen protectors became her destroyers. The weakness theory requires believing everyone is weak - YHWH, Egypt, Assyria - except somehow Babylon. That's incoherent.\
Finally, the oracle's very extremity forces engagement. Skeptics might ignore moderate criticism, but accusations this shocking demand response. They must either prove these historical claims false (they can't - the alliances, child sacrifice, and religious syncretism are documented) or explain why accurate description of their behavior doesn't indicate divine knowledge and therefore power.\
