Opus:\
You're right to demand scrutiny. Let me reconsider these claims:\
The "expensive replacement" argument is weak. People abandon gods they consider powerless all the time, and the expense could indicate desperation rather than fear. If your city is under threat and your god seems absent, paying for any alternative might seem rational, not evidence you secretly believe your god is powerful.\
The "provocation" argument similarly fails. "
\f1 \cf2 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1492 \u1463 \u1499 \u1456 \u1506 \u1460 \u1497 \u1505 \u1461 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0 \cf2 " could be Ezekiel's theological interpretation of their actions, not their intent. They might not have thought they were provoking YHWH at all - they might have thought they were simply seeking functional alternatives to a defunct deity.\
The Samaria/Sodom comparison assumes they shared Ezekiel's interpretation of those destructions. They might have attributed those falls to Assyrian military superiority or natural disaster, not divine judgment. Even if they once believed YHWH destroyed those cities, they could think his power has waned since - gods in the ancient Near East could lose power, die, or be defeated.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <notes> What actually might have logical force: - The specificity of reversal patterns if documented before events - But only if we can establish Ezekiel said this before 586 BCE - Even then, siege warfare followed predictable patterns </notes>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The prediction argument has weight only if we establish Ezekiel gave these specifics before Jerusalem's fall, and even then, temple vessels being taken and houses burned were standard conquest outcomes, not miraculous predictions.\
The "everyone is weak" argument doesn't hold either. They could reasonably believe in shifting power hierarchies among gods and nations - Babylon and Marduk are ascendant while YHWH and Jerusalem decline. That's perfectly coherent within ancient Near Eastern theology.\
What might actually work is the rhetorical trap of having to respond to specific historical claims. If Jerusalem did make these alliances, practice child sacrifice, and install foreign cults, they must explain why. But even that doesn't prove YHWH's power - just that Ezekiel accurately describes their behavior and offers one theological interpretation they must now counter with their own.\
