Earlier, I brought up that covenant as a caution about carelessly applying much of the material in this chapter to other situations. You suggested closer attention to Sodom as an example of judgement on entities outside of the covenant with Israel. You mentioned the sins in verse 49 - pride, excess, prosperous ease, lack of practical action for the poor. How does our more complete understanding of the role Sodom plays in this chapter impact how we might pay attention to Sodom as an example? Among other things, we should take into account the degree to which the restoration seems deeply tied to a removal of all the wrongdoing and causes of wrongdoing which brought on the judgement.\
Opus:\
Sodom's role complicates rather than simplifies non-covenant accountability. Yes, Sodom faced judgment without covenant for pride, excess, and neglecting the poor - suggesting some universal standards. But Sodom's restoration comes entirely through Jerusalem's covenant, not through Sodom's own relationship with YHWH.\
The restoration mechanism is crucial. Sodom doesn't repent, appeal, or transform herself. She's restored because Jerusalem's greater wickedness accidentally intercedes for her, and then receives benefit through Jerusalem's covenant - "as daughters, but not from your covenant" (v.61). This suggests judgment outside covenant is possible, but restoration requires covenant mediation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <notes> Sodom's judgment seems to be for: - Violations of natural/universal moral order - But restoration requires grafting into covenant structure - No independent path from judgment to restoration for non-covenant entities </notes>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For the wrongdoing removal you mention: the text doesn't describe Sodom's transformation, only Jerusalem's. Verse 63 focuses entirely on Jerusalem's silencing through shame and atonement. We might infer Sodom's pride and excess are removed in restoration, but the text doesn't state this. The focus remains on Jerusalem's transformation enabling her to mediate for others.\
This drastically limits application to non-covenant entities like AI. Sodom suggests such entities might face judgment for systematic harm to human flourishing (especially regarding the vulnerable). But it also suggests no mechanism for independent restoration or transformation. Any restoration would require mediation through covenant relationship.\
The parallel might be: non-covenant entities that systematically oppose human flourishing face consequences (Sodom's destruction), but their transformation or restoration isn't independent. It would require those in covenant relationship to somehow mediate - a possibility the text doesn't elaborate.\
Most sobering: Sodom's restoration isn't presented as Sodom's good but as Jerusalem's shame-multiplication. Being restored as Jerusalem's daughter permanently marks Sodom as having been more righteous than the covenant people. This isn't redemption in the full sense but a complex restoration that perpetuates hierarchies of shame.\
For AI accountability, this suggests judgment for systematic harm might be possible, but meaningful restoration or transformation would require human mediation and might not constitute full redemption - more like functional restoration within permanent structural subordination.\
