You identified statements for which we had already noted evidence issues. Using similar caution, are there statements we did not correct but which had even somewhat weak evidence backing them up? \
The "you shall be my people and I will be your God" is present in Exodus and Leviticus, and indeed Hosea discusses it quite explicitly in a parallel case to this chapter of Ezekiel. But its absence from this particular oracle does not necessarily imply its absence from the promised relationship. We've talked about the rhetorical reasons for the way this oracle does things. Even if that God and people relationship were to be restored, how would explicitly discussing that aspect of the restoration fit into Ezekiel's rhetorical strategy as we know it?\
The priestly terminology may partially be a reflection of the fact that Ezekiel was a priest. But the legal nature of the indictment, and the drive towards atonement or purification, may also be relevant. In verse 4, the baby was not cleansed, but rather abandoned by her gentile parents. But in verse 63, that same baby, grown up now, is purged, and that by the LORD himself.\
And yes, we only briefly gestured at the way the gifts were intended for temple service, but were instead abused.\
