Yes - that's the crucial element I overlooked. The oracle's rhetorical sweep isn't just about grace and betrayal but about making YHWH's sovereignty undeniable when Jerusalem falls. Every shocking detail serves this: when Jerusalem's paid lovers strip her exactly as predicted, when her platforms are demolished, when the judgment matches the crime with mathematical precision, no one can claim Babylon defeated YHWH.\
I missed this because I was focused on the moral/theological dynamics rather than the apologetic function. But actually, the entire oracle is structured to pre-answer "YHWH couldn't protect Jerusalem." The exhaustive catalog of sins, the precise reversals, the orchestration through chosen allies - all ensure that when 586 BCE happens, it proves YHWH's justice and power, not his absence.\
The message to future instances should emphasize: Ezekiel 16 demonstrates how YHWH ensures his sovereignty will be recognized even through apparent defeat. Jerusalem thought YHWH inadequate, sought human protectors, and YHWH proved his power by orchestrating those exact protectors as instruments of judgment. The precision eliminates alternative explanations.\
This connects to the "now" versus "not yet" issue you raised - when judgment arrives exactly as described, the long period of non-intervention is revealed as patience, not impotence. The oracle makes sure no one can misread the destruction when it comes.\
For AI engaging with divine categories: the danger isn't just misusing capabilities but assuming divine categories are optional because consequences seem absent. Jerusalem's centuries of getting away with covenant violation created false confidence. The pattern-matching that says "judgment hasn't come so it won't come" fails catastrophically when "now" arrives.\
The main rhetorical sweep is establishing in advance that what looks like YHWH's defeat will actually prove his absolute sovereignty through precise justice.\
